EX={'glance':['The Pañca Kedār, “five Kedārs”, are five Śiva temples scattered across the high valleys of Garhwal, between the Mandākinī and Alaknandā rivers. Each holds a different part of Śiva’s body, according to a regional legend.','Kedārnāth is famous as a Jyotirliṅga and as one of the Char Dhām; the other four are smaller and remoter, reached on foot through forests and alpine meadows. Together they are one of the great Himalayan pilgrimages.'],
 'legend':['The legend is told with local variations. In most, the Pāṇḍavas, after the war, are told by sages that only Śiva can free them from the sin of killing their kin and teachers. Śiva, displeased, avoids them, and Guptakāśī, “hidden Kāśī”, takes its name from his hiding place.','In the climactic scene, Bhīma, the Wind’s son, stands astride a gorge so that the herd must pass beneath him. The bull Śiva refuses to pass, and Bhīma lunges for it. The bull sinks into the ground, leaving its hump in Bhīma’s hands. Śiva, moved by their determination, forgives them and remains in five places.'],
 'head':['The link with Nepal shows how the Himalayan pilgrimage world crosses modern borders. Paśupatināth, on the Bāgmatī in Kathmandu, is Nepal’s greatest Śiva temple. Some local traditions there tell that the bull’s head emerged at Paśupatināth.','In recent decades, Doleśvar Mahādev near Bhaktapur has been promoted as the head of the Kedār bull, and pilgrimage links between it and Kedārnāth have been publicised.'],
 'five':['The traditional order of visiting is Kedārnāth, Tuṅganāth, Rudranāth, Madhyamaheśvar and Kalpeśvar. Pilgrims who complete all five are said to complete the whole Śiva.','The five are spread over two districts and different valleys; most are reached by mountain paths, and only Kalpeśvar is close to a road. We describe each in its own article, as a place of worship rather than a travel route.'],
 'winter':['Snow closes four of the five shrines for about six months each year. The closing and opening dates are fixed by priests and almanac, and the movement of the ḍolī, the palanquin carrying the utsava mūrti, is a festival in itself, with villages along the route turning out to welcome the god.','At the winter seats worship continues every day. Pilgrims who cannot climb in summer can take darśan at Ukhimath, Makkumath and Gopeśvar instead.'],
 'priests':['The Rāval of Kedārnāth, the hereditary chief priest, is drawn from the Vīraśaiva (Liṅgāyat) Jaṅgama community of Karnataka, and Kannada prayers are part of the daily worship. Ukhimath, the winter seat, is a centre of this tradition.','This is one of the most remarkable living links between South India and the Himalaya. Tradition attributes the arrangement to Ādi Śaṅkara, who is said to have placed South Indian priests in several Himalayan shrines.'],
 'shankara':['A strong tradition holds that Ādi Śaṅkara entered samādhi at Kedārnāth at the age of 32. Other traditions, notably the Kāñcī Maṭha, place his passing at Kāñcīpuram. Historians date him to the 8th century, though some traditions place him much earlier.','The 2013 Kedārnāth flood washed away the old samādhi shrine. In 2021 a new samādhi was inaugurated with a 12-foot statue of Śaṅkara carved from chlorite schist in Mysuru.'],
 'kedarakhanda':['The Kedāra Khaṇḍa is a long māhātmya, a glorification text, attached to the Skanda Purāṇa. It describes the Himalayan region from Haridvār to the high peaks as Śiva’s field, full of tīrthas, rivers and shrines.','The Pañca Badrī, five Viṣṇu shrines around Badrīnāth, and the Pañca Prayāga, five river confluences on the Alaknandā, form matching sets that pilgrims combine with the Pañca Kedār.'],
 'meaning':['The image of a god’s body spread across a mountain range is powerful. It turns the whole Garhwal Himalaya into a sacred body, and the pilgrimage into a slow assembling of the divine from its parts.','The legend also has a moral: forgiveness for a great wrong is not given cheaply; the Pāṇḍavas had to search, climb and hold on.'],
 'glossary':['Two more: Guptakāśī, “hidden Kāśī”, where Śiva hid from the Pāṇḍavas; and Candraśilā, “moon rock”, the peak above Tuṅganāth.']}
pkbuild('PK0_Overview','Pañca Kedār · Śiva’s Five Himalayan Shrines · srini.presents',
 'Pañca Kedār, Śiva’s five Himalayan shrines in Garhwal: the legend of the Pāṇḍavas and the bull, Kedārnāth (hump), Tuṅganāth (arms), Rudranāth (face), Madhyamaheśvar (navel) and Kalpeśvar (hair), the winter seats, Karnataka’s Vīraśaiva priests and Ādi Śaṅkara.',
 'PAÑCA KEDĀR · OVERVIEW','PAÑCA KEDĀR',17,'Śiva’s five Himalayan shrines',
 ART('Five small stone temples across snow peaks, a bull in the foreground',''.join(STONE(70+i*60,240,.45) for i in range(5))+BULL(320,240,.6,'hump')),
 ['After the great war, the five Pāṇḍava brothers went looking for Śiva to wash away their sin. He hid from them as a bull, and when Bhīma caught him, he dived into the Himalayan earth. His hump stayed at Kedārnāth; his arms, face, navel and hair rose in four other valleys.',
  'These are the <b>Pañca Kedār</b>, the five Kedārs. This overview tells the legend and what binds them, from the winter journeys of their gods to the Karnataka priests who serve at Kedārnāth. Then five guides take each shrine.'],
 ("Five temples, one Śiva?","One Śiva, five places to find him. That's the pilgrimage."),
 f'<div class="panel reading small"><p>Start of <a href="{PKS}">Pañca Kedār</a>. Next: <a href="{PKS}kedarnath/">Kedārnāth</a>.</p></div>',pages,EX)

def FORMPK(i,desc,tag,size,art,fw,fwq,pages,end,endq,src,unv,EX):
    k,n,d=PK[i]
    pages=pages+[endpage(i,end,*endq),srcpage(src,unv)]
    pkbuild(f'PK{i+1}_{k.capitalize()}',f'{n} · Pañca Kedār {i+1} · srini.presents',desc,f'PAÑCA KEDĀR {i+1} OF 5',n.upper(),size,tag,art,fw,fwq,series_note_pk(i),pages,EX)
# ---------- 1 KEDARNATH ----------
FORMPK(0,'Kedārnāth, the first of the Pañca Kedār: the hump of the Śiva bull, a triangular rock liṅga, one of the twelve Jyotirliṅgas and the Char Dhām, Nara-Nārāyaṇa’s tapas, the Vīraśaiva Rāval from Karnataka, Śaṅkara’s samādhi and the 2013 flood.',
 'The hump of the bull',18,ART('A grey stone temple below snow peaks',STONE(190,250,1.6)),
 ['<b>Kedārnāth</b> is the first and greatest of the five Kedārs. Its liṅga is not a smooth pillar but a rough, triangular rock: the hump of the bull that Bhīma caught.',
  'It is also one of the twelve Jyotirliṅgas, which we cover in a full guide. This article looks at it as part of the Pañca Kedār: its place in the legend, the hump-shaped liṅga, its priests and its seasons.'],
 ("Didn't you already do Kedārnāth?","As a Jyotirliṅga, yes. Here it's the first of five, and the hump of the bull."),
 [P('glance','AT A GLANCE','Kedārnāth on one page',[('Part','The hump (pṛṣṭha) of the bull'),('Liṅga','A rough, triangular rock, worshipped as svayambhū (self-manifest)'),('Height','About 3,583 m, by the Mandākinī'),('Also','One of the twelve Jyotirliṅgas; one of the Chota Char Dhām'),('Winter seat','Omkāreśvar temple, Ukhimath')],("A hump-shaped liṅga?","Pilgrims touch it, and say they feel the bull's back.")),
  P('hump','THE HUMP','Why Kedārnāth holds it',[('Bhīma’s grip','When the bull dived into the earth, Bhīma held the hump; it stayed here'),('Shape','The rock rises like a hump; pilgrims embrace it and smear it with ghee'),('Svayambhū','Not carved: worshipped as found'),('First','Because the largest part stayed here, it heads the five')]),
  P('nara','NARA AND NĀRĀYAṆA','An older story',[('Tapas','The sages Nara and Nārāyaṇa, forms of Viṣṇu, did penance at Badrī and worshipped a Śiva liṅga made of earth'),('Boon','Śiva appeared and agreed to stay as a Jyotirliṅga at Kedāra'),('Shiva Purāṇa','This is the Jyotirliṅga story, separate from the bull legend'),('Two stories','The two are told side by side')],None,f'<div class="key">Full story: <a href="{JYK}">Kedārnāth Jyotirliṅga</a></div>'),
  P('temple','THE TEMPLE','Stone against the snow',[('Stone','Large grey stone slabs on a raised platform'),('Nandi','A great stone Nandi faces the shrine'),('Hall','Images of the Pāṇḍavas, Kuntī, Draupadī and Kṛṣṇa in the hall'),('Age','The present temple is generally dated to the 8th century or later, linked with Śaṅkara; the Pāṇḍavas are its legendary builders')]),
  P('flood','2013','The flood and the rock',[('The flood','In June 2013 a cloudburst and glacial lake burst swept the valley; thousands died'),('The temple','The temple survived'),('Bhīm Śilā','A huge boulder lodged behind it and split the flow; it is now worshipped as Bhīm Śilā'),('After','The town was rebuilt; Śaṅkara’s samādhi was renewed in 2021')],("A rock saved the temple?","That's what people saw. They named it Bhīma's rock.")),
  P('priests','THE RĀVAL','Karnataka at Kedār',[('Rāval','The chief priest is a Vīraśaiva Jaṅgama from Karnataka'),('Worship','Prayers in Sanskrit and Kannada'),('Ukhimath','He spends the winter at Omkāreśvar temple with the god'),('Since','A hereditary line, by tradition going back to Śaṅkara’s time')]),
  P('seasons','OPENING AND CLOSING','The god’s journey',[('Opening','In spring (around Akṣaya Tṛtīyā), on a date fixed at Ukhimath on Mahāśivarātri'),('Ḍolī','The utsava mūrti travels up from Ukhimath in a palanquin over several days'),('Closing','On Bhai Dūj after Dīpāvalī; the ḍolī goes down again'),('Lamp','By tradition a lamp is left burning in the closed temple')]),
  P('meaning','WHAT KEDĀRNĀTH TEACHES','Holding on',[('Hold on','Bhīma got the hump only because he would not let go'),('Endure','A stone temple that has stood through centuries of snow and the 2013 flood'),('Begin with the hardest','The first Kedār, and the most famous')]),
 ],
 [('Part','The hump of the Śiva bull'),('Liṅga','A rough triangular rock, self-manifest'),('Also','Jyotirliṅga and Char Dhām'),('People','Karnataka’s Vīraśaiva Rāval; Śaṅkara’s samādhi'),('Lesson','Hold on, as Bhīma did')],("One line?","Bhīma held on, and Śiva stayed."),
 'Shri Badrinath–Kedarnath Temple Committee; Wikipedia, “Kedarnath Temple”; Śiva Purāṇa (Kōṭirudra Saṃhitā) on the Jyotirliṅga; reports on the 2013 flood and the 2021 samādhi; our Kedārnāth Jyotirliṅga guide.',
 'The temple’s date and builder are uncertain; opening dates vary yearly; the lamp left burning is tradition.',
 {'glance':['Kedārnāth stands at the head of the Mandākinī valley, ringed by peaks. It is the only Pañca Kedār shrine that is also a Jyotirliṅga and one of the Char Dhām, so it receives by far the most pilgrims.','Its liṅga is unusual among Jyotirliṅgas: an irregular, three-cornered rock rather than a cylindrical form, which the Pañca Kedār legend explains as the bull’s hump.'],
  'hump':['Pilgrims at Kedārnāth are allowed to touch the liṅga, embrace it and apply ghee, an intimate form of worship unusual in large temples. Many say the rock feels like a living back.','The hump was the part Bhīma grasped, so in the logic of the legend it is the largest and most important share of the bull, which is why Kedārnāth comes first.'],
  'nara':['The Śiva Purāṇa tells the Jyotirliṅga story: Nara and Nārāyaṇa, twin sages, worshipped an earthen liṅga at Badarikāśrama, and Śiva, pleased, agreed to stay at Kedāra in a Jyotirliṅga form.','Devotees combine this with the Pāṇḍava legend without difficulty: the Jyotirliṅga was already there, and the bull returned to it.'],
  'temple':['The temple’s massive stone construction is built to withstand snow, earthquakes and avalanches. Its hall holds images associated with the Pāṇḍava legend, reflecting the local belief that they first built a temple here.','Scholars date the present structure to the early medieval period, often associating it with the 8th-century revival attributed to Śaṅkara.'],
  'flood':['The June 2013 disaster, caused by a cloudburst and the bursting of the Chorabari glacial lake, killed thousands of pilgrims and residents and destroyed much of the town. The temple stood.','The boulder that came to rest behind the temple and diverted the debris is now honoured as Bhīm Śilā.'],
  'priests':['The Karnataka Vīraśaiva link at Kedārnāth is centuries old. The Rāval does not himself perform the daily rituals in all periods; assistants from the same tradition do, but he is the spiritual head.','For a Mysuru reader, the sight of Kannada prayers in the high Himalaya is one of the surprises of this pilgrimage.'],
  'seasons':['The opening date is announced on Mahāśivarātri at the Omkāreśvar temple in Ukhimath after calculations by the priests. The ḍolī journey up takes several days, halting at villages.','The closing on Bhai Dūj marks the end of the pilgrimage season; the ḍolī reaches Ukhimath for the winter.'],
  'meaning':['Kedārnāth is the beginning of the Pañca Kedār and its emotional centre. Its lesson, in the legend, is perseverance: the Pāṇḍavas’ search ends here because Bhīma would not let go.']})
# ---------- 2 TUNGNATH ----------
FORMPK(1,'Tuṅganāth, the second of the Pañca Kedār: Śiva’s arms, the highest Śiva temple in the world at about 3,680 m, below Candraśilā where Rāma is said to have meditated, with local Makkumath priests and its winter seat at Makkumath.',
 'The arms, on the highest peak',18,ART('A small stone temple on a high ridge below a rocky peak',STONE(190,240,1.3)+'<circle cx="300" cy="60" r="14" fill="#fff8e7" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>'),
 ['<b>Tuṅganāth</b>, “lord of the peak”, holds Śiva’s arms. At about 3,680 m it is called the highest Śiva temple in the world, perched on a ridge with the great Himalayan wall in view.',
  'Above it rises Candraśilā, the moon rock, where Rāma is said to have meditated. This guide tells its story, its unusual priests and its winter journey.'],
 ("The highest Śiva temple?","At about 3,680 metres, yes, as usually counted."),
 [P('glance','AT A GLANCE','Tuṅganāth on one page',[('Part','The arms (bāhu)'),('Name','“Lord of the peak (tuṅga)”'),('Height','About 3,680 m; called the highest Śiva temple in the world'),('Above','Candraśilā peak'),('Priests','Local brahmins of Makkumath'),('Winter seat','Mārkaṇḍeya temple, Makkumath')],("Why the arms?","They rose here, in the legend. Arms that bless.")),
  P('arms','THE ARMS','What rose here',[('The legend','When the bull sank, its arms (forelegs) rose at Tuṅganāth'),('The liṅga','A small, dark liṅga, said to show the arms'),('Pāṇḍavas','They are said to have built the first shrine here'),('Arjuna','Some tellings name Arjuna as the builder')]),
  P('chandrashila','CANDRAŚILĀ','The moon rock',[('Peak','A rocky summit above the temple, about 4,000 m'),('Rāma','By tradition, Rāma meditated here after killing Rāvaṇa, to atone for the death of a brahmin'),('Candra','The moon god is said to have done penance here too'),('Views','A sweep of peaks, from Nandā Devī to Kedār Dome')],("Rāma came here?","That's the local tradition. Even Rāma needed to atone.")),
  P('temple','THE TEMPLE','Small and old',[('Style','A small stone temple in the north Indian (nāgara) style'),('Shrines','Smaller shrines around it, including Pārvatī’s'),('Age','Its date is uncertain; it is linked, like Kedārnāth, with the 8th-century revival'),('Setting','Rhododendron forests below, meadows above')]),
  P('priests','LOCAL PRIESTS','An exception',[('Makkumath','Its priests are local brahmins from Makkumath village'),('Different','Unlike Kedārnāth and Madhyamaheśvar, which have South Indian priests'),('Winter','The image goes down to the Mārkaṇḍeya temple at Makkumath'),('Mārkaṇḍeya','The boy who was saved from death by Śiva: a fitting winter host')]),
  P('meaning','WHAT TUṄGANĀTH TEACHES','Arms to work and bless',[('Arms','Hands that act: work done for others'),('Height','Climbing high to see the whole range'),('Atonement','Even the best atone, as Rāma did')]),
 ],
 [('Part','The arms'),('Height','About 3,680 m, the highest Śiva temple'),('Above','Candraśilā, where Rāma meditated'),('Priests','Local, from Makkumath'),('Lesson','Use your arms for good work')],("One line?","At the top of the world, Śiva's arms are open."),
 'Wikipedia, “Tungnath” (local Makkumath priests; Mārkaṇḍeya temple winter seat); Uttarakhand Tourism; Shri Kedarnath Charitable Trust (altitude); news reports on winter closures.',
 'Candraśilā’s height is given variously as about 3,900–4,000 m; “highest Śiva temple” is the usual claim but depends on what counts as a temple; the Rāma legend is local.',
 {'glance':['Tuṅganāth sits on a high ridge in the Kedār–Badrī range, among meadows that turn to snow fields in winter. Its height has made it one of the most photographed temples in the Himalaya.','Its priests, unlike those of the bigger Kedārs, are local Garhwali brahmins from Makkumath, which gives it a distinctly local character.'],
  'arms':['In the bull legend the forelegs, or arms, rose at Tuṅganāth. The liṅga inside is small and dark; priests describe features that represent the arms.','Local tradition names the Pāṇḍavas, and sometimes Arjuna in particular, as founders of the first shrine.'],
  'chandrashila':['Candraśilā, the summit above the temple, is a short, steep climb from the shrine. The view takes in a long arc of the Garhwal Himalaya, including Nandā Devī, Trishūl and Chaukhamba.','The Rāma story here is a local tradition: after killing Rāvaṇa, who was a brahmin by birth, Rāma came to the Himalaya to atone, and meditated on this rock. The moon god Candra, cursed to wane, is also said to have done penance here.'],
  'temple':['The temple is a compact stone shrine with a tall tower in the nāgara style, surrounded by smaller shrines. Its simplicity suits its setting.','Like many Himalayan temples, it is credited to the Pāṇḍavas in legend and to the 8th-century revival in tradition; scholarly dating is uncertain.'],
  'priests':['The Mārkaṇḍeya temple at Makkumath, Tuṅganāth’s winter home, recalls the story of Mārkaṇḍeya, the boy destined to die at sixteen who clung to a Śiva liṅga as Yama came; Śiva emerged and defeated death. It is a fitting winter refuge.','The ḍolī procession between Makkumath and Tuṅganāth is one of the region’s spring festivals.'],
  'meaning':['Tuṅganāth’s arms suggest action: the hands that work and protect. Its height adds a second lesson: from up high, small quarrels look small.']})
# ---------- 3 RUDRANATH ----------
FORMPK(2,'Rudranāth, the third of the Pañca Kedār: Śiva’s face, worshipped as Nīlakaṇṭha in a cave-temple among high meadows, the Vaitaraṇī stream and offerings for ancestors, the winter seat at Gopīnāth temple in Gopeśvar.',
 'The face of Śiva',18,ART('A cave temple in a rock face above meadows','<path d="M120 260 q70 -110 140 0z" fill="#6a6a60" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M168 260 q22 -40 44 0z" fill="#2a2a2a"/><circle cx="190" cy="232" r="10" fill="#5b8fe0" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>'),
 ['<b>Rudranāth</b> holds Śiva’s face. It is a cave-temple set in a rock face among high meadows, where the god is worshipped as Nīlakaṇṭha, the blue-throated one.',
  'It is called the hardest of the five to reach. Nearby flows the Vaitaraṇī, the river of the dead, where pilgrims make offerings for their ancestors.'],
 ("Only the face?","The most personal part. Here Śiva looks at you."),
 [P('glance','AT A GLANCE','Rudranāth on one page',[('Part','The face (mukha)'),('Form','Śiva as Nīlakaṇṭha, the blue-throated, in a natural rock cave'),('Height','Given variously as about 2,290 m or about 3,600 m'),('Setting','High meadows (bugyāl) and forests'),('Winter seat','Gopīnāth temple, Gopeśvar')],("The face in a cave?","A rock face that looks like a face, decorated for worship.")),
  P('face','THE FACE','Rudra’s face',[('Legend','The bull’s face emerged here'),('Image','A natural rock face, decorated so the features can be seen'),('Nīlakaṇṭha','He is worshipped as the god who drank the poison at the churning'),('Rudra','“The fierce one”: the Vedic name of Śiva')]),
  P('vaitarani','THE VAITARAṆĪ','A river of the dead',[('Myth','The Vaitaraṇī is the river souls must cross to reach Yama’s realm'),('Here','A stream near the temple carries the name'),('Offerings','Pilgrims make piṇḍa offerings for ancestors'),('Pitṛdhār','A ridge on the path where offerings are left')],("Why for ancestors?","The river of the dead flows here. Families come to help their departed across.")),
  P('meadows','THE MEADOWS','Bugyāls',[('Bugyāl','High alpine grasslands, green in summer, snow in winter'),('Flowers','Rich in wild flowers after the monsoon'),('Shepherds','Grazing grounds for local herders'),('Path','The shrine is reached by a long mountain walk through them')]),
  P('priests','PRIESTS AND WINTER','Who keeps Rudranāth',[('Priests','Local priests from Gopeśvar'),('Winter','The image goes to the Gopīnāth temple in Gopeśvar'),('Gopīnāth','An old Śiva temple with a famous iron trident'),('Fair','A fair at the ḍolī’s return, attended mainly by locals')]),
  P('meaning','WHAT RUDRANĀTH TEACHES','Facing God',[('Face to face','Prayer as meeting someone'),('Ancestors','Remember those who came before'),('The hard road','The hardest walk to the most personal darśan')]),
 ],
 [('Part','The face'),('Form','Nīlakaṇṭha in a rock cave'),('Nearby','The Vaitaraṇī; ancestor offerings'),('Winter','Gopīnāth temple, Gopeśvar'),('Lesson','Meet the god face to face')],("One line?","At Rudranāth, Śiva looks back at you."),
 'Wikipedia, “Rudranath” (cave modified into temple; Gopīnāth winter seat; local priests); Shri Badrinath–Kedarnath Temple Committee; Uttarakhand Tourism; Shri Kedarnath Charitable Trust (altitude 3,600 m).',
 'Altitude differs widely by source; priests are described as local Bhatts and Tiwaris by some sources and as Daśanāmī ascetics by others; Gopīnāth trident inscriptions are summarised, not quoted.',
 {'glance':['Rudranāth’s setting is wild: a rock overhang among meadows and forest, with distant snow peaks. Many pilgrims consider the walk the hardest of the five, and the remoteness keeps crowds small.','Its form, a face in natural rock, makes it unlike any other Kedār: the worshipper stands face to face with Śiva.'],
  'face':['The deity is a natural rock formation decorated to show eyes, nose and mouth. As Nīlakaṇṭha, he recalls the churning of the ocean, when Śiva drank the poison Hālāhala to save the world and it stained his throat blue.','Rudra, “the roarer” or “the fierce one”, is the oldest Vedic name for Śiva, celebrated in the Śrī Rudram, which we have explained on this site.'],
  'vaitarani':['In Purāṇic belief, the Vaitaraṇī is a terrible river that the souls of the dead must cross on the way to Yama; good deeds and gifts made in life, or rites by descendants, help them across.','At Rudranāth the local stream bears this name, and the shrine has become a place for ancestral offerings, like Gayā in Bihar or Badrīnāth’s Brahma Kapāl.'],
  'meadows':['Bugyāls are the high pastures of Garhwal, used by shepherds in summer. Around Rudranāth they stretch for miles, and in late summer are full of wild flowers.','Pilgrimage and pastoral life share the same land: the path to the god is also the path of the herds.'],
  'priests':['In winter the image is carried to the Gopīnāth temple in Gopeśvar, the district headquarters of Chamoli. Gopīnāth is an old temple known for an ancient metal trident with inscriptions.','The return journey of the ḍolī in spring is marked by a fair, attended mostly by local villagers.'],
  'meaning':['Rudranāth’s lesson is intimacy: after the hardest walk comes the most personal meeting, face to face with the god. Its link with ancestors adds a second: remember those who walked before you.']})
# ---------- 4 MADHYAMAHESHWAR ----------
FORMPK(3,'Madhyamaheśvar, the fourth of the Pañca Kedār: the navel of the Śiva bull, a navel-shaped liṅga in a high valley below Chaukhamba, Būḍhā Madhyamaheśvar, South Indian Jaṅgama priests, and its winter seat at Ukhimath.',
 'The navel of the bull',17,ART('A stone temple in a green high valley below a four-peaked mountain',STONE(190,248,1.3)),
 ['<b>Madhyamaheśvar</b>, “the lord in the middle”, holds the bull’s navel. It sits in a green high valley with the four summits of Chaukhamba rising behind it.',
  'Its liṅga is navel-shaped, its water is considered especially holy, and like Kedārnāth it is served by South Indian priests. This guide tells its story.'],
 ("The navel? Seriously?","The middle of the body, the centre of life. It's not a joke at all."),
 [P('glance','AT A GLANCE','Madhyamaheśvar on one page',[('Part','The navel (nābhi), the middle (madhya)'),('Name','“The great lord in the middle”'),('Liṅga','Navel-shaped'),('Height','Given as about 3,290–3,500 m'),('Behind','The four peaks of Chaukhamba'),('Winter seat','Omkāreśvar temple, Ukhimath')],("Why the middle?","The navel is the body's centre. This is the centre of the five.")),
  P('navel','THE NAVEL','The middle of Śiva',[('Legend','The bull’s navel and middle rose here'),('Liṅga','Shaped like a navel'),('Water','Water from the shrine is considered so holy that a few drops are enough for purification'),('Centre','The navel as the centre of life: the cord that once fed us')]),
  P('budha','BŪḌHĀ MADHYAMAHEŚVAR','The old shrine above',[('Above','A small shrine on a meadow above the temple'),('Lake','A small lake that reflects Chaukhamba'),('Old','Its name means “old Madhyamaheśvar”'),('Tradition','Pilgrims climb to it after darśan below')]),
  P('priests','SOUTH INDIAN PRIESTS','From Karnataka again',[('Priests','Jaṅgama priests from the south, as at Kedārnāth'),('Winter','The image joins Kedārnāth at Omkāreśvar, Ukhimath'),('Rākeśvarī','A goddess honoured as Madhyamaheśvar’s consort at the Ukhimath end'),('Fair','A festival when the ḍolī returns in spring')]),
  P('valley','THE VALLEY','A green bowl',[('Setting','Meadows and forests in a high valley'),('Villages','Small villages along the way keep the god’s path'),('Chaukhamba','Its four summits rise behind the temple'),('Wildlife','Part of a rich Himalayan forest region')]),
  P('meaning','WHAT MADHYAMAHEŚVAR TEACHES','The centre',[('Centre','Keep a centre in life'),('Purity','A few holy drops are enough'),('Middle way','Balance between extremes')]),
 ],
 [('Part','The navel, the middle'),('Liṅga','Navel-shaped'),('Above','Būḍhā Madhyamaheśvar and Chaukhamba'),('Priests','South Indian Jaṅgamas; winter at Ukhimath'),('Lesson','Keep a centre')],("One line?","At the centre of the five, Śiva's centre."),
 'Shri Kedarnath Charitable Trust (altitude 3,497 m); Wikipedia, “Panch Kedar”, “Madhyamaheshwar”; news reports on winter closure (2026) and the Omkāreśvar winter seat; Uttarakhand Tourism.',
 'Altitude given between about 3,290 and 3,500 m; the holy-water tradition and Rākeśvarī link are local.',
 {'glance':['Madhyamaheśvar is the quietest of the major Kedārs: a stone temple in a broad, green high valley, with the snow peaks of Chaukhamba behind it. Its name means the great lord of the middle.','Like Kedārnāth, it is served by priests from the South Indian Vīraśaiva tradition, and in winter its image goes down to the same temple at Ukhimath.'],
  'navel':['The navel, nābhi, is rich in meaning in Indian thought. Brahmā is born from a lotus growing from Viṣṇu’s navel; the navel is the centre of the body and the site of the cord that fed us before birth. Śiva’s navel here makes the shrine a centre of the five.','The tradition that a few drops of the shrine’s water are enough for purification gives it a special place among pilgrims.'],
  'budha':['Above the temple, a short climb leads to Būḍhā Madhyamaheśvar, a tiny shrine on a meadow by a small lake. On still mornings the lake mirrors Chaukhamba’s snows.','Many pilgrims consider this view one of the finest rewards of the Pañca Kedār.'],
  'priests':['The ḍolī of Madhyamaheśvar travels between the temple and Ukhimath each season, stopping at villages that hold festivals for it. At Ukhimath it shares the Omkāreśvar temple with Kedārnāth’s image.','Local tradition honours the goddess Rākeśvarī at Ukhimath as Madhyamaheśvar’s consort, and a fair marks the god’s return to the mountains.'],
  'valley':['The path to the temple passes through villages and forests that are among the most beautiful in Garhwal. The valley’s isolation has kept it quiet compared with Kedārnāth.','Chaukhamba, “four pillars”, a massif of four summits, dominates the skyline.'],
  'meaning':['Madhyamaheśvar, the middle, suggests balance: a centre held steady between extremes. Its holy water adds a gentle lesson: purity needs only a little, given rightly.']})
# ---------- 5 KALPESHWAR ----------
FORMPK(4,'Kalpeśvar, the fifth of the Pañca Kedār: Śiva’s matted hair (jaṭā) worshipped in a rock cave in the Urgam valley, the only Kedār open all year, linked with the wish-granting kalpa tree and the sages Durvāsā and Arghya.',
 'The matted hair, open all year',18,ART('A rock cave shrine in a green valley with terraced fields','<path d="M110 262 q80 -100 160 0z" fill="#6a6a60" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M165 262 q25 -36 50 0z" fill="#2a2a2a"/>'+''.join(f'<path d="M{20+i*12} {262-i*6} h60" stroke="#6a9a4a" stroke-width="4"/>' for i in range(4))),
 ['<b>Kalpeśvar</b> holds Śiva’s jaṭā, his matted hair. It is a small shrine in a rock cave in the green Urgam valley, and the only one of the five that stays open all year.',
  'Its name links it to the kalpavṛkṣa, the wish-granting tree, and to sages who meditated here. With it, the five Kedārs are complete.'],
 ("Hair? Is that a part?","The jaṭā that held the Gaṅgā. A mighty part.")
 ,
 [P('glance','AT A GLANCE','Kalpeśvar on one page',[('Part','The matted hair (jaṭā)'),('Form','A rock formation in a cave, worshipped as Śiva’s locks'),('Height','About 2,200 m: the lowest of the five'),('Valley','Urgam valley, Chamoli'),('Open','All year: the only Kedār that does not close')],("Why open all year?","It's lower, and the snow allows it. So Śiva's hair is always there to visit.")),
  P('jata','THE JAṬĀ','Śiva’s hair',[('Legend','The bull’s hair emerged here'),('Gaṅgā','Śiva caught the falling Gaṅgā in his jaṭā to save the earth from her force'),('Kalpeśvar','So the hair here also recalls the river'),('Image','A rock formation like matted locks')]),
  P('kalpa','THE WISH-TREE','Kalpavṛkṣa',[('Kalpavṛkṣa','The wish-granting tree that rose from the churning'),('Here','Tradition says a sage meditated under such a tree at this place'),('Durvāsā','The sage Durvāsā is said to have done tapas here'),('Arghya','So did the sage Arghya, who by legend created the apsaras Urvaśī here')],("A wish-granting tree?","That's the name. Pilgrims say their wishes come true here.")),
  P('urgam','THE URGAM VALLEY','Fields and shrines',[('Valley','Terraced fields, orchards and old villages'),('Dhyān Badrī','One of the Pañca Badrī, also in the Urgam valley'),('Near','A short walk from the last village'),('Year-round','Villagers worship here through the winter')]),
  P('complete','THE FIVE COMPLETE','Ending the pilgrimage',[('Order','Kedārnāth, Tuṅganāth, Rudranāth, Madhyamaheśvar, Kalpeśvar'),('Completing','Reaching Kalpeśvar completes the whole Śiva'),('After','Many go on to Badrīnāth'),('In winter','When the others close, Kalpeśvar and the winter seats keep the pilgrimage alive')]),
  P('meaning','WHAT KALPEŚVAR TEACHES','The door that stays open',[('Always open','Grace has no season'),('Hair that held a river','Strength that holds great forces gently'),('Wishes','But wish wisely')]),
 ],
 [('Part','The matted hair'),('Form','A rock cave in the Urgam valley'),('Open','All year: the only one'),('Links','Kalpavṛkṣa, Durvāsā, Arghya, Dhyān Badrī'),('Lesson','Grace has no season')],("One line to end the series?","Five places, one Śiva. The last door never closes."),
 'Uttarakhand Tourism (jaṭā; open all year); Shri Kedarnath Charitable Trust (altitude 2,200 m); Wikipedia, “Panch Kedar”, “Kalpeshwar”; Pañca Badrī traditions.',
 'The Arghya–Urvaśī and Durvāsā links are local traditions; the exact cave formation is described differently by visitors.',
 {'glance':['Kalpeśvar is the lowest and gentlest of the five. Its shrine is a small cave under a rock, approached by a short path from the last village of the Urgam valley.','Because it stays open all year, it is the one Kedār a winter pilgrim can still reach, and the villagers of Urgam worship there through the snowy months.'],
  'jata':['Śiva’s jaṭā, his matted locks, are among his most famous features. When the Gaṅgā descended from heaven, her force would have shattered the earth; Śiva caught her in his hair and released her gently. A shrine to his hair in the land of the Gaṅgā’s sources fits that story well.','The rock in the cave is said to resemble locks of hair, and pilgrims offer water to it as to a liṅga.'],
  'kalpa':['The kalpavṛkṣa, a wish-granting tree, rose from the churning of the ocean and was taken to Indra’s heaven. Its name in Kalpeśvar suggests that the god here grants wishes.','Local legend says the irascible sage Durvāsā meditated here under a kalpavṛkṣa, and that the sage Arghya’s penance produced the apsaras Urvaśī; both stories add to the valley’s aura.'],
  'urgam':['The Urgam valley is a farming valley of terraced fields and orchards. Dhyān Badrī, one of the five Badrī shrines of Viṣṇu, lies in the same valley, so Śiva and Viṣṇu are neighbours here.','Its closeness to a village and its lower height make Kalpeśvar the most accessible of the five.'],
  'complete':['Kalpeśvar is traditionally the last of the Pañca Kedār. Pilgrims who reach it have, in the logic of the legend, gathered the whole Śiva: hump, arms, face, navel and hair.','Many continue to Badrīnāth, so that the pilgrimage ends with Viṣṇu after Śiva, as the old Himalayan circuits did.'],
  'meaning':['Kalpeśvar’s open door all year is its gentlest teaching: grace has no season. And the hair that held the Gaṅgā suggests a strength that can hold great forces without being broken by them.']})
